<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dayan Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1464–<a href="/years/1517">1517</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Batu Möngke · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dayan Khan, Batu Möngke, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a> khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a> who reunited the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in the late 15th and early 16th centuries, with the help of his queen Mandukhai. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> and rebellious chiefs and divided the Mongols into six tümen ruled by his sons. His descendants founded the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> khanates and the Tümed realm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dayan-khan.jpg" alt="Dayan Khan" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Avishay · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BATMUNKH_DAYAN_KHAN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dayan Khan was born Batumongke, the son of Bayanmongke, the Bolkhu jinong or crown prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a> clan, and Shiker of the Uriyangkhai. His father and his uncle Manduul Khan came to blows, and Bayanmongke fled and was murdered. Manduul's taishi Ismayil took Shiker and her property. To save the child, Shiker gave him to the Bakhay family to nurse, and he was cared for by commoners and minor nobles because of his Borjigin blood. When Manduul Khan died suddenly in 1467, Mandukhai Khatun, his widow, adopted the boy and had him treated for a serious illness.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Mandukhai, a direct descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>, had the boy enthroned as khan under the title Dayan, and later married him. She kept great influence over court and army. Together they reunified the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a>, who had revolted against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a> rule since the 14th century. In 1483 they led armies against Ismayil, who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Hami</a> and was killed there. By 1495 Batumongke had won the Three Guards, tributaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, and incorporated them into his Six tumens.</p><p>His relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> broke down after one of his envoys, sent to arrange open trade, was killed at the Ming court. Mongol raids on the borderlands became almost constant. In 1500 the khan and Mandukhai moved to the Eight White Yurts in Ordos and attacked Ningxia. A Ming counterattack the next year nearly captured him, and he withdrew to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kherlen-river">Kherlen River</a>, but large raids continued through 1507. In 1517 he moved against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, and the Ming forces of the Zhengde Emperor held him off at the Battle of Yingzhou.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The three Right Wing tumens (Ordos, Tümed and Yöngshiyebü) invited Dayan Khan to rule them. His son Ulusbold was killed in a riot while being enthroned as jinong, and Dayan Khan was first defeated at Turgen Stream. In 1510 he crushed the Right Wing tumens and killed the Ordos elder Mandulai. He enthroned his son Barsubolad as jinong in 1513 and abolished old Yuan titles such as taishi and chingsang.</p><p>He organised the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> into six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumens</a>, the Left Wing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a>, Chahar and Uriankhai and the Right Wing of Ordos, Tümed and Yöngshiyebü, each serving as both a military unit and a tribal administration. He divided them among his sons, which created a decentralised but stable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a> rule for a century. Civil war among Chinggisid princes was unknown until the reign of Ligden Khan (1604-34). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a> and Ligden Khan were among his descendants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dayan Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pushed west by <b>Dayan Khan</b> and Altan Khan, the Oirats moved towards Dzungaria in the 16th century and adopted Gelug Buddhism.</blockquote><a href="/state/four-oirat">Four Oirat</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Dayan Khan</b> reunited the eastern Mongols around 1500 into six tumens, and from the 1570s Tibetan Buddhism spread under Altan Khan.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the descendants of Geresenje, son of <b>Dayan Khan</b> of the Northern Yuan, the princes of eastern Khalkha along the Kherlen river took the title Setsen Khan in the late 16th century, forming one of the three great divisions of Khalkha alongside the Tüsheet Khanate and Zasagt Khanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/setsen-khanate">Setsen Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Altan Khan, a grandson of <b>Dayan Khan</b> of the Northern Yuan, became leader of the Tümed tumen in the 1530s and turned it into the strongest power among the eastern Mongols.</blockquote><a href="/state/tumed-altan-khan">Tumed (Altan Khan)</a></li><li><blockquote>The Khalkha Mongols were given by <b>Dayan Khan</b> to his son Geresenje, whose descendants divided them.</blockquote><a href="/state/tusheet-khanate">Tüsheet Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Dayan Khan</b> of the Northern Yuan gave the Khalkha Mongols of northern Mongolia to his youngest son Geresenje, whose sons divided them into appanages.</blockquote><a href="/state/zasagt-khanate">Zasagt Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1498" aria-label="Mongolia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7032 544 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dayan Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7482 814h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7482 814h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1498">What was here in 1498? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
